msgid "On the portfolio"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:159
msgid "Read `loans.csv` into a list of rows - one comprehension - and select from it. Each row is a list of strings, in the order of the header. The ids of the stage 3 loans (`stage` is column 10), and the outstanding amounts in thousands of the USD loans:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:179
msgid "The columns are still **text** - `row[10] == \"3\"`, not `== 3`. Convert where you need a number (`float(row[7])`), as in session 2."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:182
msgid "`CASE WHEN` inside: `a if condition else b`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:184
msgid "A conditional expression (session 2) can be the expression of a comprehension. Then every item goes into the list, with one of two values:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:194
msgid "The position of `if` decides what it does"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:195
msgid "`if` **at the end** filters: fewer items. No `else` is allowed there."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:196
msgid "`a if c else b` **at the start** chooses a value: the same number of items. The `else` is required."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:212
msgid "Generator expressions: `sum(... for ...)`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:214
msgid "When you only need the **total**, you do not need the list. Write the comprehension without the square brackets, inside the call:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:229
msgid "This is a **generator expression**: it gives the values one by one to `sum`, and never builds a list in memory. It works with any function that takes many values: `sum`, `min`, `max`, `any`, `all`, `sorted`, `set`, `\", \".join(...)`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:233
msgid "`sum(1 for ... if ...)` is SQL `COUNT(*) ... WHERE ...`. Another way to count: `True` is `1` (session 2), so `sum(int(row[9]) > 0 for row in rows)` gives the same."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:236
msgid "Set comprehensions: `SELECT DISTINCT`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:238
msgid "Curly brackets make a **set** - the unique values (session 3):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:252
msgid "Dictionary comprehensions, with `{key: value for ...}`, are in session 6."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:254
msgid "When not to use a comprehension"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:256
msgid "A comprehension is for **building a new list**. Use a normal loop when:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:258
msgid "you only want an effect, not a list - for example printing: `[print(x) for x in items]` works, but builds a useless list of `None`;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:260
msgid "the logic needs several steps or several `if` / `elif` branches - put it into a function and call the function in the comprehension, as with `dpd_bucket` above, or write the loop;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:263
msgid "the line becomes too long to read in one look (more than about 80 characters)."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:265
msgid "Advanced: two `for` in one comprehension"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:266
msgid "Several `for` parts run like nested loops, the first one outside:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:280
msgid "Read it as the loops it replaces: `for month in monthly:` then `for x in month:`. More than two `for` parts are hard to read - write the loops."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:284
msgid "Advanced: `map` and `filter`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:285
msgid "You will meet them in other people's code. `map(function, items)` calls the function for every item; `filter(function, items)` keeps the items for which the function returns something true. Both give their values one by one - wrap them in `list()` to see them. A comprehension does the same job and reads better:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:301
msgid "In pandas, `map` comes back: `series.map(function)` or `series.map(dictionary)` - the Python version of a SAS format (sessions 6 and 15). `functools.reduce` combines the items into one value, two at a time; for sums, minimums and maximums the built-in functions are clearer."
msgstr ""

