#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:337
msgid "2. Checks and extremes: `any`, `all`, `min`, `max`"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:339
msgid "`any` and `all`: questions about the whole list"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:341
msgid "`any(values)` is `True` if **at least one** value is true."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:342
msgid "`all(values)` is `True` if **every** value is true."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:344
msgid "With a generator expression, they ask a question about every row - the checks you run on data before a report:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:358
msgid "`row[0].startswith(\"L\")` is `True` when the text starts with `\"L\"` - a string method; session 7 has them all. In SQL these are `EXISTS (SELECT ... WHERE ...)` and `NOT EXISTS (... WHERE NOT ...)`. Both stop as soon as the answer is known: `any` at the first true value, `all` at the first false one."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:363
msgid "Empty lists"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:364
msgid "`any([])` is `False` - nothing is true. `all([])` is `True` - nothing is false. A check with `all` on an empty file \"passes\". Check the number of rows too."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:372
msgid "`min` and `max` with `key`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:374
msgid "Session 4 showed `key=` with `sorted`, `min` and `max`. With rows, `key` says which column to compare - and you get back the **whole row**, not only the value:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:389
msgid "A trap: numbers as text"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:390
msgid "`max([\"9\", \"10\", \"100\"])` is `\"9\"`: strings compare letter by letter (session 2). Convert first - `key=float` or `float(...)` in the generator. Without `key`, `max(rows)` compares whole rows as text - the first column first - and gives the largest **id**, not the largest loan."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:401
msgid "`default=`: when the list can be empty"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:403
msgid "`max([])` is a `ValueError`. When a filter may leave nothing, give a `default`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:412
msgid "`sum`, `len` and the average"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:426
msgid "This is SAS `PROC MEANS` for one variable. The module `statistics` (session 10) adds `median`, `stdev` and more; pandas gives all of them in one `describe()` (session 14)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:430
msgid "Change the checks block above: is there a loan with `outstanding` greater than `amount`? Is every `stage` one of `\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, `\"3\"`? Predict the answers first."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:434
msgid "3. `zip` and `enumerate`"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:436
msgid "`zip`: several lists side by side"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:438
msgid "`zip` takes several lists and gives **tuples**: the first items together, then the second items, and so on:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:452
msgid "Columns become rows. A common use: two lists of the same months, compared month by month:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:464
msgid "And a list with itself, one step apart - the change from month to month (in SAS: `LAG`):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:474
msgid "`outstanding[1:]` starts one month later, so each pair is (the month before, this month)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:477
msgid "A trap: the shortest list wins"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:479
msgid "`zip` stops at the end of the **shortest** list - without an error. A missing value in one list silently drops the last row:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:489
msgid "Check the lengths first when the lists come from different places: `len(loan_ids) == len(amounts)`."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:492
msgid "`enumerate`: the number of each item"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:494
msgid "Sometimes you need the **position** of each item, not only the item. The C-style way loops over the indexes with `range(len(...))`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:503
msgid "The Python way is [`enumerate(iterable, start=0)`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#enumerate): it gives pairs `(number, item)`, so the `for` takes the number and the item with two names. `start=1` numbers from 1 - a rank, or the line number of a file (the line 1 is the header):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:517
msgid "The line numbers of the rows with a missing amount - in SAS: `_N_`:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:528
msgid "Advanced: more of `zip` - transpose, `strict=True`, used-up iterators"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:529
msgid "**Transpose a table.** With the `*` of session 4, `zip(*rows)` turns a list of rows into a list of **columns** - a small SAS `PROC TRANSPOSE`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:545
msgid "**`strict=True`.** Since Python 3.10, `strict=True` turns \"the shortest list wins\" into an error:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:555
msgid "**Used-up iterators.** `zip`, `enumerate`, `map` and `filter` do not build a list. They are **iterators**: they give the values one by one, **once**. The second time they are empty - if you need the values twice, keep a list: `pairs = list(zip(...))`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:569
msgid "In a DataFrame, the columns are already side by side - no `zip` is needed. The month-to-month change is `shift` (session 16):"
msgstr ""

