#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:960
msgid "6. Copies of a table"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:962
msgid "A shallow copy shares the rows"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:964
msgid "Session 3: `rows.copy()`, `rows[:]` and `list(rows)` make a **new list**. But the items in it - here the rows - are the **same** objects. The copy is *shallow*:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:978
msgid "The outer list is copied; the inner lists are shared. Changing a **cell** shows in both; adding or removing a **row** does not."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:981
msgid "Copy the rows too"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:983
msgid "A copy that is safe to change copies every row - a comprehension again:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:993
msgid "Make it before you change cells of a table that you still need in its original form - like `data backup; set loans; run;` before an update in SAS."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:996
msgid "Advanced: `copy.deepcopy`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:997
msgid "`copy.deepcopy` copies the list **and** every list inside it, at every level - also lists inside the rows:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1011
msgid "Better: rows that cannot change"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1013
msgid "If the rows are **tuples**, nobody can change a cell - not by mistake, not through a shared reference. To change a value, you build a new row:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1024
msgid "The comprehensions of this lecture always build new rows - `r + [value]`, `(r[0], r[2])` - so they never change the input. That is the safe default for data work."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1027
msgid "pandas and copies"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1028
msgid "In pandas 3 (and in 2.2 with copy-on-write switched on), a selection behaves like a copy: changing it never changes the original DataFrame. Session 15 shows it."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1032
msgid "**Check yourself.** What does this print?"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1042 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1169 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1227
msgid "Answer"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1043
msgid "`[[1, 2], [7, 4]]`. `b[0] = [9, 9]` puts a new list into `b` only (the outer lists are different). `b[1][0] = 7` changes the inner list that `a` and `b` share."
msgstr ""

