#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1097
msgid "This part is not covered in class. It is for reading at home: two tools that SAS users miss in plain Python - `RETAIN`, `LAG` and BY groups - and a way to read files that do not fit in memory."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1101
msgid "`itertools`: running totals, `LAG` and BY groups"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1103
msgid "The module `itertools` has building blocks for loops. Three of them replace common patterns of the SAS DATA step."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1106
msgid "**`accumulate` - a running total (`RETAIN`).** In SAS: `retain total 0; total + x;`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1116
msgid "**`pairwise` - the value and the one before (SAS `LAG`).** Since Python 3.10:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1126
msgid "It is the same as `zip(xs, xs[1:])` from section 3, without the copy of the list."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1128
msgid "**`groupby` - BY-group processing (SAS `FIRST.` / `LAST.`).** `groupby` cuts a **sorted** list into groups of equal keys - like a SAS DATA step with `BY product`:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1145
msgid "Sort first"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1146
msgid "`groupby` starts a new group every time the key **changes**. On unsorted data the same product appears in many small groups - exactly like a SAS `BY` without `PROC SORT`, except that Python does not stop with an error."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1151
msgid "Two more, again with their SAS names: `islice(iterable, n)` takes the first `n` items of any iterator (`OBS=n`), and `chain(a, b)` reads several lists one after another (`SET a b;`)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1162 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1221
msgid "**Exercises**"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1164
msgid "With `accumulate`, find the first month in which the running total of `[1200, 800, 1500, 900, 1100]` goes over 3000."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1166
msgid "With `groupby`, count the loans per currency in `loans.csv`. Why must you sort first?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1179
msgid "Sort by currency, then `groupby(rows, key=lambda r: r[3])` and `len(list(group))`: EUR 361, USD 39. Without sorting, EUR and USD rows alternate, and you get many small groups instead of two."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1184
msgid "Generator functions: `yield`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1186
msgid "A function with `yield` instead of `return` is a **generator function**. Each call of `next` runs it until the next `yield`, and the function **waits** there, with its local variables, until the next value is asked for:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1200
msgid "This is useful for big files: a generator that reads one row at a time, converts it and gives it on. The whole file is never in memory - only the current row:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1217
msgid "A generator, like `zip` or `map`, is used up after one pass - here each line calls `loan_rows(...)` again, which opens the file again. A SAS DATA step works this way too: it reads one observation at a time."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1223
msgid "Write a generator `late_loans(path, limit)` that yields only the `loan_id` of the loans with `days_past_due` greater than `limit`. Count them for `limit=90`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1225
msgid "What does `list(g)` print the second time, for `g = loan_rows(\"data/loans.csv\")`?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1240
msgid "`[]` - the generator is used up after the first `list(g)`."
msgstr ""

